WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
you have to  buckle up  you need to commit yourself  to working hard  not just hard but twice as hard  it's walking through an experience  with the people  who really  are misguided they're misinformed  about who you are  and so they're fitting you in  in their terms  i went to university to learn how to  become a better actor and to last longer  in the business because i was well aware  of indigenous actors at that time you  know playing leathers and feathers  when i was growing up there weren't any  role models out there except for maybe  harry smith from six nations  why can't i be the king of england why  can't i be the king of france you know  why don't people see me that way  i was one of the generation that kind of  plowed the road for these young kids who  said if he can do it then we can do it  too and now native people are coming out  of the woodwork they're writing their  own scripts producing their own films  getting on television networks  the industry should continue down the  path that it's on one step after another  is  stay on the path  that is  paved  with compassion  with empathy  with faith  with love  anywhere we walk  is the opportunity  to  be a piece of truth  i encourage young indigenous artists to  keep asking questions learn from the  best and learn from the up and coming  know who you are  i think that's the great challenge in  life  keep your head on straight and  keep your head above what's going on  don't get immersed into  the quagmire of misery and suffering and  the poisons that lurk around  just stay away from that stuff  be your own person and be steadfast  if you build your audience that's what  will attract business they don't really  care how cute your song is i'm sorry to  say but the people really pulling the  strings want to know whether it's going  to sell and bring in money to support  their company an ally  is not a leader  so when you're ally you're side by side  that's a little tendency that we have to  put up with in terms of people from  outside of our culture feeling that oh  well i'm better educated or i'm smarter  i know that answer and i'm pushing  forward  and  we don't really need that we have to  acknowledge the truth  and the great spiritual path to healing  can often include pain  but we have to acknowledge the truth  before we rush to reconciliation  the best thing to do is to educate  yourself there's so much information  available at your fingertips on the  internet do yourself a favor go seek it  out because you will learn more by  asking your own questions don't rely on  getting answers from the first  indigenous person you see because you  know we get asked so much it's  exhausting and it's also very personal  because we're going through trauma we  don't want  sympathy we want understanding  we want  to understand what what had happened  and what has happened and what still is  going on deny the narrative that this  was some isolated incident  [Music]  now  channel your feelings of anger toward  action  uncover undocumented graves across  canada's residential schools  get it done  there has to be tremendous work done on  the part of settler culture to come  towards us  and that is an act of reconciliation

